import pf.Html
import ../../Ui/Heading
import ../../Ui/Code
import ../../Generated/Sources
import ../../Generated/Paths
import ../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../Ui/TableWrap
import ../../Ui/Versus

## File-based routing, as SvelteKit has it: this site's own pages are routed this way.
meta = {
	title: "Routing",
	description: "File routing in Roc Solid: pages, layouts, the error page and endpoints are files under routes/, params come from directory names, a directory's middleware guards the routes in it, links navigate without a reload, and rocx writes a typed path for every page and a typed function for every endpoint.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "files", label: "The files" },
		{ id: "page", label: "A page" },
		{ id: "layouts", label: "Layouts" },
		{ id: "params", label: "Params, and typed links" },
		{ id: "error", label: "Where no page is" },
		{ id: "endpoints", label: "Endpoints: +server.roc" },
		{ id: "middleware", label: "Middleware: what runs, in what order" },
		{ id: "navigating", label: "Navigating without a reload" },
		{ id: "written", label: "What rocx writes" },
		{ id: "router", label: "The router" },
		{ id: "openapi", label: "The OpenAPI document" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Not yet" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="Routing"
				title="A page is a file"
			>
				An app's URLs come from the files under its <code>routes/</code> directory, as in SvelteKit. A
				<code>+page.rocx</code> is a page at the path its directory names. The <code>+layout.rocx</code>
				files above it wrap it, a <code>+server.roc</code> answers HTTP requests at its path (a page's own
				under <code>routes/api/</code>, at the page's path), and <code>+error.rocx</code> is shown where
				nothing answers. rocx writes a typed path for every page and a
				typed function for every endpoint, so a link to a page that doesn't exist, or a call to an endpoint
				that doesn't, fails to compile.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="files">The files</Heading>
			<p>These are this site's route files. Every page you can open here is one of them:</p>
			<Code title={Sources.route_tree.file} source={Sources.route_tree.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} plain />
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>File</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>routes/+page.rocx</code></td><td>the page at <code>/</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>routes/blog/+page.rocx</code></td><td>the page at <code>/blog</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>routes/blog/[slug]/+page.rocx</code></td><td>the page at <code>/blog/anything</code>, given <code>{"params.slug"}</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>+layout.rocx</code></td><td>wraps every page in its directory and below it</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>routes/+error.rocx</code></td><td>what a browser sees at a path nothing answers (404), and when a page fails</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>routes/blog/+server.roc</code></td><td>the endpoints at <code>/blog</code>: <code>get!</code>, <code>post!</code>, <code>put!</code>, <code>patch!</code> and <code>delete!</code>; <code>events!</code>, a stream of events; <code>channel!</code>, a channel pages subscribe to; <code>respond!</code>, every other method</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>routes/admin/+middleware.roc</code></td><td>the middleware around every page, endpoint and channel in <code>admin/</code> and below it (<a href="#middleware">below</a>); <code>routes/+middleware.roc</code> runs around every request</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>routes/(members)/…</code></td><td>a group: a directory in brackets adds nothing to the path, so files at different paths share its middleware and layout</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>routes/.well-known/…</code></td><td>routed, as RFC 8615 asks; any other directory whose name starts with a dot is skipped</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				Each directory's name is one part of the path, in lower case (<code>blog</code>,
				<code>getting-started</code>, <code>feed.xml</code>), or a param in brackets (<code>[slug]</code>).
				It may start with an underscore, for a path another service names (<code>_verify</code>, named
				<code>verify</code> in <code>Api</code> and <code>Paths</code>). <code>rocx build</code> refuses any
				other name, and says why.
			</p>

			<Heading id="page">A page</Heading>
			<p>
				A route file has no module header: it starts with its imports, then its definitions. A page
				defines <code>view!</code>, the component that renders it. It may also define <code>meta</code>, a
				record its layouts are given. On this site, <code>meta</code> holds the title, the description and
				the table of contents on the right:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.route_page.file} source={Sources.route_page.rocx} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong><code>view!</code> is given the page's params:</strong> <code>{"view! = |{ params }| …"}</code>,
					a record of strings, or of the <a href="#params">types its handlers give them</a>
					(<code>{"{}"}</code> on a page without params). Write <code>|_|</code> when the
					page needs none. A page is an <a href={"${Paths.components}#kinds"}>effectful component</a>: its body
					runs once, and it may make signals.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Its data comes from endpoints.</strong> A page isn't given the request. It reads where it
					is with <code>{"Router.location!({})"}</code>, and reads its data from an endpoint with
					<code>Endpoint.read!</code>, which the server answers while it renders the page
					(<a href={Paths.calls}>talking to the server</a>). What middleware knows, such as who is signed in,
					reaches a page the same way: through an endpoint behind that middleware
					(<a href="#middleware">below</a>).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Imports are relative to the file</strong>, as everywhere in rocx. This one is two directories
					down, so the site's own components are under <code>../../Ui</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A route file can define components of its own</strong>
					(<code>{"Board := [].{ view! = … }"}</code>), as any <code>.rocx</code> file can, and use them as
					<code>{"<Board />"}</code>. Put them before the file's definitions or after them, not between:
					rocx writes them as types beside the page's module, so their <code>view!</code> isn't taken for the
					page's (<a href={"${Paths.components}#title"}>an example</a>). They can use the file's other
					definitions, as the page can; a name a component defines itself is its own.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="layouts">Layouts</Heading>
			<p>
				A <code>+layout.rocx</code> wraps every page in its directory and below it. Layouts nest, the
				outermost first. The root one, <code>routes/+layout.rocx</code>, renders the whole document:
				<code>{"<html>"}</code>, <code>{"<head>"}</code> and <code>{"<body>"}</code>. An app without one gets
				a plain document. A layout's <code>view!</code> is given:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li><strong><code>children</code></strong>: the page, or the next layout in;</li>
				<li>
					<strong><code>params</code></strong>: its own params, the ones its directory names
					(<code>routes/[lang]/+layout.rocx</code> gets <code>lang</code>; the root layout gets
					<code>{"{}"}</code>);
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong><code>meta</code></strong>: the page's <code>meta</code>. Every page under a layout that
					takes it must give it the same type, and <code>rocx build</code> reports a page whose
					<code>meta</code> has other fields, or none. Where no page is, the error page's <code>meta</code>
					stands in.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				When the visitor moves to another page, a layout stays, with its state, as long as its file and its
				own params are the same (<a href="#navigating">navigating</a>). This site has one layout, the
				document around every page. It reads the location where it uses it, not once at its top, so it is
				kept:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.route_layout.file}, in part"} source={Sources.route_layout.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />

			<Heading id="params">Params, and typed links</Heading>
			<p>
				A directory in brackets is a param: <code>routes/blog/[slug]/+page.rocx</code> is the page at any
				<code>/blog/…</code>, and its <code>view!</code> is given <code>{"params : { slug : Str }"}</code>. A
				param is a string, decoded from the URL, unless a handler's annotation gives it another type. A fixed part is
				tried before a param in the same place, so <code>/blog/new</code> can be a page of its own beside
				<code>/blog/[slug]</code>. A directory may also name a file, with dots between its letters or digits:
				<code>routes/sitemap.xml/+server.roc</code> answers <code>/sitemap.xml</code>, and its names leave the
				dot out (<code>Api.get_sitemap_xml</code>). This page is from the app the tests build:
			</p>
			<Code title="test/e2e/routes/routes/blog/[slug]/+page.rocx" source={Sources.route_fixture_page.rocx} />
			<p>
				rocx also writes <code>Paths</code>: a string for every page without params, and a function of its
				params for every page with them, which encodes each param for the URL. Link to pages through it.
				Then a link to a page that doesn't exist, or one with the wrong params, fails to compile, and the
				compiler says so at your line (<code>{"Paths.blog_slug({ slg: \"x\" })"}</code>: <em>Maybe slg should
				be slug?</em>). <code>Paths.all</code> lists the pages without params.
			</p>
			<Code title={"What rocx writes for that app: ${Sources.route_fixture_paths.file}"} source={Sources.route_fixture_paths.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<strong>Typed params.</strong> A handler's annotation may give a param a type other than
				<code>Str</code>: a number, <code>Bool</code>, or a union of tags written out
				(<code>{"[Draft, Live]"}</code>). The router reads the path's part as that type before anything runs,
				and a part that doesn't read is no match, so <code>/api/count/five</code> is a 404, as a path nothing
				answers is, and the handler never parses it. The type is the directory's: every handler under it
				gives the param the same one (rocx build says where two differ), and the pages and layouts there
				are given it too, as <code>Paths</code> and <code>Api</code> take it. A page whose endpoints are under
				<code>api/</code> says its own, as a type in the page, <code>{"Params : { id : I64 }"}</code>: rocx
				never reads a page's types from another directory. This site's count of
				<a href={"${Paths.calls}#events"}>events</a> is typed so:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.endpoint_count.file} source={Sources.endpoint_count.rocx} />
			<p>This site's sidebar is made of them, each part's pages in groups under a heading:</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.route_nav.file}, in part"} source={Sources.route_nav.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />

			<Heading id="error">Where no page is</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>routes/+error.rocx</code> is the app's error page. A browser that asks for a path nothing
				answers gets it, inside the root layout, with the status 404; any other client gets a 404 as plain
				text. A page that fails, where nothing inside it catches the failure with an
				<a href={"${Paths.components}#waiting"}><code>{"<Errored>"}</code></a>, shows the error page too. When
				the failure is a read its endpoint refused with a 4xx status, the page keeps that status (an endpoint
				answering <code>NotFound</code> makes the page a 404); anything else is a 500. The error page's
				<code>view!</code> is given <code>status</code> and <code>problem</code>, why it failed as text. An app
				without one gets a plain one. This site's lists the pages of the part of the docs the path is in (try
				<a href="/nowhere">/nowhere</a>, or <a href="/platform/nowhere">/platform/nowhere</a>):
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.route_error.file} source={Sources.route_error.rocx} />

			<Heading id="endpoints">Endpoints: +server.roc</Heading>
			<p>
				A <code>+server.roc</code> (plain Roc; <code>+server.rocx</code> if it has markup) answers HTTP
				requests at its directory's path. Each method it answers is a handler named after it:
				<code>get!</code>, <code>post!</code>, <code>put!</code>, <code>patch!</code> or <code>delete!</code>.
				A handler is given a record of the request, the path's params, the context its middleware handed on
				and, for a method with a body, the body, read as the type its annotation says. It answers
				<code>Ok(result)</code>, sent as JSON with the status 200, or <code>Err(refusal)</code>:
				<code>BadRequest(why)</code> is a 400, <code>NotFound</code> a 404, and
				<code>{"Answer(Server.redirect(…))"}</code> an answer of its own. This one answers the form on this
				site's <a href={Paths.members_join}>joining page</a>, and sets a cookie:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.members_join_server.file} source={Sources.members_join_server.rocx} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Bodies.</strong> A body is read when it is JSON (<code>application/json</code>) or a form
					(URL-encoded, or <code>multipart/form-data</code> with files). A field that is missing, or can't be
					read as its type, is a 400 that names it. Any other body, such as an upload of raw bytes, isn't
					read: the handler takes <code>{"{}"}</code> as its body and reads <code>request.body</code>, the
					bytes as they came.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Checked bodies.</strong> A route file may name the rules its method's body is read with,
					beside the handler: <code>post_body = Signup.form</code>, a form of
					<a href={"${Paths.ecosystem_forms}#endpoint"}>packages/forms</a>. The body, a form or JSON, is read
					and checked by them before the handler runs, and the handler's <code>body</code> is their record.
					What doesn't pass is a <code>422</code> with the issues as JSON, and <code>/openapi.json</code>
					documents the body with the rules' schema (D-093).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Three more names.</strong> <code>events!</code> answers a GET that asks for events
					(<code>accept: text/event-stream</code>) with a stream (<a href={"${Paths.calls}#events"}>events</a>).
					<code>channel!</code> defines a channel that pages subscribe to
					(<a href={"${Paths.calls}#channels"}>channels</a>). <code>respond!</code>, given the request, the
					params and the context, answers every method the file doesn't name with a
					<code>Server.Response</code> of its own: a page mounted from elsewhere, a JSON-RPC endpoint.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A path is a page or an endpoint.</strong> A page's endpoints, what it reads and what its
					forms post to, live under <code>routes/api/</code>, at the page's path: the page at
					<code>/members</code> reads <code>GET /api/members</code>. One URL gives one answer, which no cache
					can mix up. A directory may still hold both: then a browser's GET (one that accepts HTML) gets the
					page, anything else the endpoints', and every answer there says <code>Vary: Accept</code>. A method
					nothing answers is a 405 that lists the ones that are (<code>Allow</code>).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Typed at both ends.</strong> Each handler must be annotated, and rocx copies the annotations
					into <code>Generated/Api.roc</code>, the app's contract: a function for each endpoint, with its
					method, URL and types, which any module may import (a component, a job, an actor).
					<code>Generated/Handlers.roc</code> binds each one to its handler, on the server only.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A form posted without JavaScript</strong> doesn't get the result or the refusal as JSON,
					which the browser would show as a bare page. It goes back to the page it came from, with a flash
					that page reads (<a href={"${Paths.calls}#forms"}>forms</a>).
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				<a href={"${Paths.calls}#endpoints"}>Talking to the server</a> shows the rest: pages reading
				endpoints as they render, handlers sending them, forms posting to them, and pages following their
				events, with this site's endpoints live.
			</p>

			<Heading id="middleware">Middleware: what runs, in what order</Heading>
			<p>
				Middleware is code that runs around the answer to a request. It is given the request, the context
				so far, and <code>next!</code>, what comes after it. It can answer the request itself (a guard
				answers with a redirect or a 401), pass it on with more context (who is signed in), or change the
				answer that comes back (add a header). Guards, sessions, security headers and logs are all
				middleware, and <code>a.then(b)</code> runs <code>a</code>, then <code>b</code>.
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.middleware_type.file}, in part"} source={Sources.middleware_type.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				Middleware goes by folder, as routes do. A <code>+middleware.roc</code> names the middleware that runs
				around every page, endpoint and channel in its directory and the directories below it, and
				nothing else runs any. The one in <code>routes/</code> itself is the app's, around every request
				it answers (a plugin's route, a path nothing has). This site's adds two headers to every answer:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.site_root_middleware.file} source={Sources.site_root_middleware.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				Below it, each runs inside the ones above, outermost first. What a middleware hands on is the
				<code>context</code> the next one is given, and the handlers inside all of them
				(<code>{"get! = |{ context, .. }| …"}</code>). The members' page, <code>/members</code>, and its
				endpoint, <code>/api/members</code>, sit in a group, <code>routes/(members)/</code>: a directory in
				brackets adds nothing to the path, so the page and its endpoint under <code>api/</code> share one
				guard, and the joining page, <code>routes/members/join/</code>, sits outside it with its own. A
				change another site's page sends (a POST, PUT, PATCH or DELETE) is refused before any of them,
				unless the app says <code>cross_site: Allowed</code> in its <code>App.program</code>. This site's
				middleware:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.site_middlewares.file} source={Sources.site_middlewares.rocx} />
			<Code title={Sources.site_members_middleware.file} source={Sources.site_members_middleware.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<Versus>
				<Code title={"${Sources.members_page.file}, in part"} source={Sources.members_page.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
				<Code title={Sources.members_server.file} source={Sources.members_server.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			</Versus>
			<p>
				A request to <code>/members</code> goes through <code>routes/+middleware.roc</code>'s, then the
				group's <code>Middlewares.member</code>, then the page. The answer goes back out through them, and
				picks up the headers on its way. The page itself isn't given the context. It reads its endpoint,
				<code>GET /api/members</code>, as it renders, and the server answers that read through the
				endpoint's route, behind the same middleware, so the handler gets the member's name. A handler's
				<code>Endpoint.send!</code> of the app's own endpoint, and an MCP tool made from one, go the same
				way: what guards an endpoint guards every way to it. <code>rocx routes</code> lists every route, the
				file that answers it, and what runs around it, outermost first:
			</p>
			<Code title="A terminal" source={Sources.route_list.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} plain />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>The context is typed.</strong> <code>member</code> is a
					<code>{"Middleware({ facts : Str }, { facts : Str, member : Str })"}</code>: it is given the
					context the one above it handed on, and hands on one with <code>member</code> added. So the
					members' endpoint can read <code>context.member</code>, and an endpoint that read it outside
					that group wouldn't compile.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A new file is guarded by where it is.</strong> A page or endpoint added to the group
					runs behind its middleware with no line of its own. A route file can't name middleware:
					<code>rocx build</code> refuses <code>middleware = …</code> in a page, an endpoint, a layout or the
					error page, and says which <code>+middleware.roc</code> to move it to.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Only some requests.</strong> <code>{"Middleware.when(Server.changes, guard)"}</code> runs
					<code>guard</code> for the requests that change something, and lets reads by: a rate limit on a
					form's posts, say.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Try it with <code>just dev</code>.</strong> <a href={Paths.members}>/members</a> sends you to
					join; join, and it greets you by the name you gave. The static copy of this site has no server to
					ask, so these pages aren't in it.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="navigating">Navigating without a reload</Heading>
			<p>
				A click on a link to another of the app's pages doesn't reload the browser's page, as with Solid
				Router's links. The runtime already running in the page fetches the new page's HTML document, as a
				reload would get it: from the server, through its middleware, with the page's reads answered as it
				rendered and any redirect followed (or, on a static copy, the file the build wrote). The app's wasm
				already holds every page's code, so it renders the new page from that document's answers and
				<code>{"<NoHydration>"}</code> parts, and asks for nothing else. An app needs no changes to get this,
				and every link on this site works this way.
			</p>
			<p>
				The old page stays on screen until the document is in. Then the address is pushed to the history,
				the new page is made, and its <code>{"<head>"}</code> is brought in line with the document's (a
				stylesheet already loaded stays). The page scrolls to the top, or to the element the link's
				<code>#hash</code> names; the focus goes where a reload would put it (an <code>autofocus</code>
				element, or the start of the page); and a screen reader hears the new title. Back and forward
				render their entry's page the same way and restore its scroll. A document fetched in the last ten
				seconds is used again.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Write</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>{"<a href={Paths.blog_slug({ slug })}>"}</code></td><td>renders the page in place. Any plain link does: in markup, in Markdown, inside a <code>{"<NoHydration>"}</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"<a href={…} replace noscroll>"}</code></td><td>replaces the history entry instead of pushing one; keeps the scroll where it is</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"rel=\"external\""}</code>, <code>target</code>, <code>download</code>, a modifier key</td><td>left to the browser, as on any page: a reload, a new tab, a download</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"navigate!(Paths.blog_slug({ slug }))"}</code></td><td>in a handler: goes to that page once the handler is done. <code>{"navigate_with!(to, { replace, scroll })"}</code> takes Solid Router's options (<code>{"import pf.Router exposing [navigate!, navigate_with!]"}</code>)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"Router.is_routing!({})"}</code></td><td>whether a navigation is on its way, tracked: a progress bar's <code>{"class:on={…}"}</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"Router.location!({})"}</code></td><td>where the page is, <code>{"{ path, query }"}</code>, tracked: a hole that reads it follows each navigation</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"a[aria-current=\"page\"]"}</code>, <code>{"a[aria-current=\"true\"]"}</code></td><td>every link to one of the app's pages says where the page is, as Solid Router's <code>{"<A>"}</code> did: <code>page</code> at its path (and its query, if it has one), <code>true</code> at a path under it (never <code>/</code>). Kept right as you navigate; a link that writes its own <code>aria-current</code> is left alone. Style with CSS</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"search = use_search_params!({})"}</code></td><td>the query: <code>{"search.get!(\"days\")"}</code> and <code>{"search.all!()"}</code> read it, tracked; <code>{"search.set!([(\"days\", \"30\")])"}</code>, in a handler, merges into it (<code>{"\"\""}</code> takes a name out) and changes it in place (<code>{"import pf.Router exposing [use_search_params!]"}</code>)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"<Navigate href={Paths.sign_in} />"}</code></td><td>sends the visitor elsewhere as the page renders: a 303 from the server, or, in the browser, a navigation that replaces the history entry (<code>{"import pf.Navigate"}</code>)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"use_before_leave!(|leave| …)"}</code></td><td>in a component: asked before each navigation away; it may stop it (<code>{"leave.prevent!()"}</code>) and let it go later (<code>{"leave.retry!(Bool.True)"}</code>). Solid Router's <code>useBeforeLeave</code> (<code>{"import pf.Router exposing [use_before_leave!]"}</code>)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"<form action=\"/search\">"}</code></td><td>a GET form: navigates in place to its URL, with its fields as the query</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"<html data-rocx-navigation=\"off\">"}</code></td><td>every link reloads the page instead; <code>"explicit"</code> navigates in place only for a link marked <code>data-rocx-link</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"data-rocx-preload=\"off\""}</code>, <code>{"\"viewport\""}</code></td><td>on a link or an element around it (<code>{"<html>"}</code> for the whole app): never prefetch; or prefetch as the link comes into sight, as well as on hover, focus and touch</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>{"<html data-rocx-transitions>"}</code></td><td>makes each navigation a View Transition, the browser's cross-fade, where the browser has them and the visitor hasn't asked for less motion. This site has it, with its bar and sidebar given a <code>view-transition-name</code> so they stay put while the page changes</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Layouts are kept.</strong> Below the root layout, each layout above the page, and the page
					itself, is a level. A level is made anew only when its file or its own params change. From one
					blog post to the next, the blog's layout stays, and so do its sidebar's scroll, an open menu and its
					signals; only the page is made again. One case breaks this: a level that reads where it is once, at
					the top of its body (<code>{"path = Router.location!({}).path"}</code>), would keep showing the old
					location, so the runtime makes it anew on every navigation, and a dev build says so in the console.
					Read the location where you use it, in markup or a memo, and the level is kept. A kept level's
					<code>{"<NoHydration>"}</code> parts are taken from the new page's document.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A change of the query alone happens in place.</strong> A link to <code>?days=30</code>,
					<code>{"search.set!"}</code> or <code>{"navigate!(\"?days=30\")"}</code> fetches no document. The page
					and its layouts stay, and whatever read the query reads it again. A read whose URL changed asks its
					endpoint, behind that endpoint's middleware, and shows its last value meanwhile. Back goes to the
					previous query. What only the server renders (a <code>{"<NoHydration>"}</code>) doesn't follow the
					query, since the browser has no code for it.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>The server decides, as on a reload.</strong> The document is fetched as a page's GET, with
					<code>x-rocx-navigate: 1</code> so that a middleware that counts views can tell it from a reload. A
					guard that refuses or redirects does so here too, and a redirect lands at its address. The browser
					reloads instead when the target isn't one of this app's pages (a plugin's page, a file, a path no
					page has), when the answer isn't a document of this build (a text 401, a crash, a new deploy's
					page: the page says which build it is from), or when it can't be fetched.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A link is fetched before it is clicked.</strong> When the pointer rests on a link, the
					keyboard focuses it or a finger touches it, its document is prefetched (with
					<code>x-rocx-navigate: preload</code>), so the click has nothing left to ask. A prefetch renders a
					page on the server that may never be seen: a middleware that counts views should leave those out,
					and an app whose pages are costly to render can turn prefetching off.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Leaving unsaved work can ask first.</strong> A component that holds unsaved work registers a
					listener with <code>{"use_before_leave!"}</code>. It runs before each navigation away from the page:
					a link, <code>{"navigate!"}</code>, a form's redirect, a change of the query, back and forward. It
					is told where the visitor is going (<code>to</code>, <code>from</code>, and for back and forward how
					far, <code>delta</code>), and may stop the navigation: the page and its address stay (back and
					forward, which had already changed the address, put it back). It can hold the navigation while the
					app asks its own question, and a "Leave without saving" button lets it go with
					<code>{"leave.retry!(Bool.True)"}</code>, skipping the listeners (<code>{"Bool.False"}</code> asks
					them again). A listener decides from what the page already has, not by asking the server, and goes
					away with its component. A real page load (another site, a reload, the tab closed) can't be held
					that way: while a listener would stop one, the browser asks the visitor in its own words. The
					<a href={Paths.examples_tasks}>tasks example</a>'s task page does this.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Page scripts run on a load only.</strong> A navigation doesn't run a
					<code>{"<script>"}</code> the new page renders. Script that sets itself up on <code>rocx:ready</code>
					should do so again on <code>rocx:navigated</code>, an event on the document with the URL and how long
					the navigation took (this site's inspectors do).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>What the old page started stops with it.</strong> Its effects' cleanups run, its timers and
					event streams stop, and its channels are left, though a channel the next page subscribes to as well
					keeps its stream.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="written">What rocx writes</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>rocx build</code> turns the routes into Roc modules under <code>Generated/</code>. You never edit
				them, but knowing what they are helps when you read the compiler's reports:
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>What it holds</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>ShowcasePage.roc</code>, <code>ApiMembersServer.roc</code>…</td><td>each route file, as a module named after its path</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Paths.roc</code></td><td>a path for every page (<a href="#params">typed links</a>)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Api.roc</code></td><td>the app's contract: a function for each endpoint and channel, with its method, URL and types</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Handlers.roc</code></td><td>the server's only: each endpoint bound to its handler</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Routes.roc</code></td><td>the server's router: the answer to a request (<a href="#router">below</a>)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Root.roc</code></td><td>the pages as one component, in their layouts: what the server renders and the browser hydrates and navigates</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>App.roc</code></td><td>the server's program, which <code>main.roc</code> finishes with <code>App.program</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Styles.roc</code></td><td>the app's stylesheet, from the components' scoped <code>{"<style>"}</code>s</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				Roc names a module after its file, and <code>+page</code> isn't a valid name, so each route file
				becomes a module of its own, named after its path (<code>routes/showcase/+page.rocx</code> is
				<code>ShowcasePage</code>). rocx keeps the lines where they were: it puts the module's first line on
				the blank line after the imports (or, with no blank line, at the start of the first definition's
				line). Line <var>N</var> of the module is line <var>N</var> of the route file, so the compiler's
				reports point at your lines:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.route_page_generated.file} source={Sources.route_page_generated.rocx} />

			<Heading id="router">The router</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>Generated/Routes.roc</code>'s <code>respond!</code> is the answer to a request. It splits the
				path into parts and tries each route, the fixed paths before the params. At a match, it answers
				with the page inside its layouts (for a browser's GET), the endpoint for the method, or a 405. Where
				nothing matches, it answers with the error page:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.route_routes.file}, in part"} source={Sources.route_routes.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>App.program</code> answers every request with it, behind the app's middleware
				(<code>routes/+middleware.roc</code>), and the GETs the server makes while it renders a page go the
				same way. A route in a directory with a <code>+middleware.roc</code> above it is wrapped in
				<code>Middleware.run!</code>, one inside the other, outermost first, and the handler is given what
				the innermost passed on. The members page and its endpoint run behind their group's:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.route_members.file}, in part"} source={Sources.route_members.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />

			<Heading id="openapi">The OpenAPI document</Heading>
			<p>
				An app can serve an OpenAPI document (3.2) of its endpoints, for a client generator or for people
				exploring the API. It lists each path and its params (a <a href="#params">typed param</a> with its
				type's schema), each method with the doc comment above its handler, and the JSON Schemas of what
				each endpoint is sent and answers. A schema is the handler's
				type as the compiler has it: this site's <code>{"GET /api/shout/{word}"}</code> answers
				<code>{"{ word : Str, shouted : Str, bytes : U64 }"}</code>, and its document says so. The endpoints
				pages read are in it too, since a page's data is an endpoint like any other. A body a form can send
				(a record of text, numbers, yes-or-no, choices and lists of them) is listed three ways:
				<code>application/json</code>, <code>application/x-www-form-urlencoded</code> and
				<code>multipart/form-data</code>. The form's schema is the same, except that a yes-or-no or a list
				may be left out, as a form leaves out a box nobody ticked. A body checked by rules
				(<a href="#endpoints"><code>post_body</code></a>) is listed the same three ways, with the rules'
				schema: what they require, and their lengths, patterns and ranges.
			</p>
			<h3>Turning it on</h3>
			<p>It is off unless the app turns it on, with <code>openapi</code> in its <code>App.program</code>:</p>
			<Code title="main.roc" source={"program = App.program({\n\tinit!: |started| Ok({ facts: started }),\n\topenapi: Page(Swagger),\n})"} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th><code>openapi:</code></th><th>The app serves</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>Off</code>, or no <code>openapi</code></td><td>neither</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Document</code></td><td><code>GET /openapi.json</code>: the document</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>Page(Swagger)</code>, <code>Page(Scalar)</code></td><td>that, and <code>GET /openapi.html</code>: the document in Swagger UI, or in Scalar</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>It is behind the app's middleware</strong> (<code>routes/+middleware.roc</code>), as every
					answer is: a guard there keeps these from strangers too.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>The page loads its viewer from jsDelivr</strong>, pinned to one version and checked
					against its hash, and tells it not to call home: no validator for Swagger UI; no telemetry, fonts,
					developer tools or agent for Scalar. Bundling a viewer instead would add 2 to 5 MB to every app's
					server.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>An app that writes its own <code>program</code></strong>, instead of using
					<code>App.program</code>, puts <code>OpenApi.middleware(Page(Swagger), Api.openapi)</code> at the end
					of its middleware chain, as <code>App.program</code> does, or asks
					<code>OpenApi.respond</code> before its routes.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				This site serves both: <a href="/openapi.html">its API, in Swagger UI</a>, and
				<a href="/openapi.json">the document</a>.
			</p>
			<h3>Who makes it, and when</h3>
			<p>
				Neither platform makes it: rocx writes Roc that asks for it, the compiler works it out, and the
				server only sends it.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>When</th><th>What runs</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<td><code>rocx build</code></td>
							<td>rocx (<code>rocx-cli/Routes.roc</code>), as it writes the router</td>
							<td>
								Writes Roc, and knows no types. <code>Generated/Api.roc</code> gets <code>openapi</code>: each
								path, its params and its methods, with their comments, and for each endpoint a call that asks
								<code>OpenApi</code> for the schemas of its body and its answer. It also gets <code>tags</code>,
								every name in the app's code that could be a tag. <code>Generated/App.roc</code> serves the
								document, as <code>openapi</code> says.
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>roc build</code>, the server's</td>
							<td>the Roc compiler</td>
							<td>
								Infers each endpoint's types and writes, for each, a parser for the format
								<code>OpenApi.Schema</code>, as it writes one for <code>Json</code> to read the endpoint's
								body. Since <code>openapi</code> is a constant, the compiler works out its value as it
								compiles: the server's binary holds the document as text.
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td><code>GET /openapi.json</code></td>
							<td>the server (native)</td>
							<td>Sends that text, if the app turned it on, behind its middleware, like every answer.</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				The browser's build (wasm) has none of it: its copy of <code>Api</code> is the same contract (each
				endpoint's method, URL and types), without the document. What rocx writes for this site, in part:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.openapi_api.file}, in part"} source={Sources.openapi_api.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<h3>A parser that writes</h3>
			<p>
				Roc has no reflection: no code can ask what a type is. But the parser the compiler writes for a type
				isn't tied to JSON. It works with any format: it calls the format, shape by shape, to read each part
				(a record, its next field, a string, a number), and it hands a record's format the names of its
				fields. <code>OpenApi.Schema</code> is a format that reads nothing. For each part it is asked to
				read, it writes that part's schema, as <code>Json</code> would write that part, and gives back a value
				nothing looks at. <code>OpenApi.body</code> asks the compiler for the parser of what the endpoint is
				sent, for <code>Schema</code>, and runs it:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.openapi_format.file}, in part"} source={Sources.openapi_format.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				So for the answer of <code>{"GET /api/shout/{word}"}</code>, <code>{"{ word : Str, shouted : Str, bytes : U64 }"}</code>,
				the parser and <code>Schema</code> go:
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>The parser calls</th><th><code>Schema</code> writes</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>parse_record_start</code></td><td><code>{"{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{"}</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>parse_record_field</code>, given the names <code>bytes</code>, <code>shouted</code>, <code>word</code>: it hands back <code>bytes</code></td><td><code>{"\"bytes\":"}</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>parse_u64</code>, as <code>bytes</code> is a <code>U64</code></td><td><code>{"{\"type\":\"integer\",\"minimum\":0}"}</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>parse_record_field</code> and <code>parse_str</code>, for <code>shouted</code>, then <code>word</code></td><td><code>{",\"shouted\":{\"type\":\"string\"}"}</code>, and <code>word</code>'s</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>parse_record_field</code>, with no name left: it says <code>Done</code></td><td><code>{"},\"required\":[\"bytes\",\"shouted\",\"word\"]}"}</code></td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				A tag union is the one shape whose parser doesn't say what it holds: it asks the format for a tag's
				name, then reads that tag's payloads. So rocx gives the document <code>tags</code>, every name in the
				app's code that could be a tag (its own, and its packages' and platforms'), and <code>Schema</code>
				offers each in turn. The parser turns down a name that isn't one of the union's; for one that is, it
				reads the payloads, and <code>Schema</code> writes their schemas. <code>{"[Red, Rgb(U8, U8, U8)]"}</code>
				is a string, <code>"Red"</code>, or an object, <code>{"{ \"Rgb\": [r, g, b] }"}</code>, as <code>Json</code>
				writes them.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>A GET with events</strong> (<code>events!</code>) answers <code>text/event-stream</code>
					too: each event's data is JSON, with its schema, and an <code>end</code> event ends the stream.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A refusal</strong> is every method's default answer: its status, and why, as text.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A type of your own</strong> (<code>{"Email := Str.{ parser_for : _ }"}</code>) is described as
					<code>Json</code> reads it. Write its parser for any format (<code>encoding.parse_str</code>), not for
					JSON alone, and the document can describe it too.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="limits">Not yet</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>No static pages with params.</strong> This site's static copy writes the pages without
					params (<code>Paths.all</code>) and behind no directory's middleware but <code>routes/</code>'s. A
					page with params would need a list of the values to write.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>One error page</strong>, the app's, at the root of <code>routes/</code>: <code>rocx build</code>
					refuses a <code>+error.rocx</code> anywhere else.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>No rest params</strong> (<code>[...path]</code>).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>The OpenAPI document</strong> calls every JSON field required, describes a type that holds
					itself (a tree) only so many levels deep, and has no <code>$ref</code>s. Its title is the app's
					directory's name. A tag union's tags come from the names in the app's code, until Roc's parsers say
					which tags a union has, as they say a record's fields.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
